From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: fall back to software csum/GSO
 when the parser cannot find L3

The EDMA TX path enables the inline checksum engine by setting the
ip_csum_en/l4_csum_en bits in TX descriptor word5 (edma_tx_fill_desc()),
but never programs the L3/L4 offset fields in word7 and ignores the skb's
csum_start/csum_offset. The hardware therefore locates the headers with
its own parser, which only appears to understand Ethernet framing with at
most one VLAN tag.

Anything that inserts further bytes ahead of the L3 header moves the
headers out from under that parser, and the checksum is written at the
wrong offset: every CHECKSUM_PARTIAL frame then leaves with a corrupted
checksum and is dropped by the peer. A DSA tagger is the case that bites
here -- DSA_TAG_PROTO_RTL8_4, used by RTL837x switches, pushes eight
bytes between the source MAC and the EtherType. Disabling TX checksum
offload with "ethtool -K <conduit> tx off" makes the problem disappear,
which confirms the diagnosis.

Testing against netdev_uses_dsa() would be enough to fix that case, but
it is both too broad and too narrow: too broad because a tag_8021q
tagger inserts an ordinary VLAN header the parser handles perfectly
well, so masking there needlessly costs the conduit its checksum and TSO
offload; too narrow because any other head insertion would be missed.

Compare instead where the L3 header will actually be with where the
parser will look for it, and fall back to software checksumming and GSO
when they differ. That keeps full offload for ordinary framing and
covers every head-inserting tag protocol without naming one. A trailer
tagger would leave the offset unchanged and is not covered; none is in
use here.

Three details decide where L3 actually ends up:

Hardware segmentation uses the same parser, and edma_tx_fill_desc()
enables TSO from skb_is_gso() alone, independently of ip_summed. A GSO
skb that is not CHECKSUM_PARTIAL -- a GRO-coalesced frame being
forwarded, which is the ordinary WAN-to-LAN path -- would otherwise keep
hardware segmentation and be sliced at the misplaced offsets, which is
worse than a bad checksum: the result is structurally malformed segments
rather than frames a peer simply drops. Run the check whenever either
checksum offload or segmentation is in play.

An offloaded VLAN tag is not in the data when this callback runs:
validate_xmit_vlan() follows netif_skb_features(). Unless the hardware
will insert it, it is pushed inside afterwards and shifts L3 by a
further VLAN_HLEN, so count it on both sides. A single offloaded tag
then still keeps full offload, while a frame that ends up double-tagged
-- beyond what the parser follows -- does not.

Only an 802.1Q tag is assumed to be one the parser follows. No TPID
register is programmed anywhere in this driver, so whether the hardware
also recognises 802.1ad is unknown; assuming it does would keep offload
on a frame whose L3 the parser never reaches, while assuming it does not
merely costs offload on a configuration nothing here uses.

Documentation/networking/dsa/dsa.rst requires a conduit driver whose
offload engine does not understand the switch tag in use to "correctly
fall back to software checksum when the IP header is not where the
hardware expects".

Note the "Ethernet plus at most one VLAN" premise is taken from observed
behaviour, not from documentation. The logic above is fail-safe given
that premise; it deserves a hardware A/B over VLAN, QinQ and TSO before
being relied on.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c | 90 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 89 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 /* EDMA port initialization, configuration and netdevice ops handling */
 
 #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
+#include <linux/if_vlan.h>
 #include <linux/net.h>
 #include <linux/netdevice.h>
 #include <linux/of_net.h>
@@ -191,10 +192,97 @@ static int edma_port_change_mtu(struct n
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static netdev_features_t edma_port_feature_check(__maybe_unused struct sk_buff *skb,
+static netdev_features_t edma_port_feature_check(struct sk_buff *skb,
 						 __maybe_unused struct net_device *netdev,
 						 netdev_features_t features)
 {
+	bool vlan_parseable = true;
+	int expected = ETH_HLEN;
+	unsigned int nvlan = 0;
+	int l3_off;
+
+	/* The TX checksum/TSO engine ignores skb->csum_start: no L3/L4
+	 * offsets are programmed into the TX descriptor, so the hardware
+	 * locates the headers with its own parser, which understands only
+	 * Ethernet with at most one VLAN tag. Anything inserting further
+	 * bytes ahead of the L3 header -- a DSA tagger such as RTL8_4, for
+	 * instance -- moves the headers out from under that parser, and the
+	 * checksum is then written at the wrong offset, corrupting every
+	 * offloaded frame.
+	 *
+	 * Rather than hard-code which tag protocols are safe, compare where
+	 * the L3 header will actually be with where the parser will look for
+	 * it, and fall back to software checksumming and GSO when they
+	 * differ. Ordinary framing -- including the single VLAN a tag_8021q
+	 * tagger inserts -- keeps full offload.
+	 *
+	 * Documentation/networking/dsa/dsa.rst requires a conduit driver
+	 * whose offload engine cannot parse the tag in use to fall back to
+	 * software checksumming.
+	 */
+
+	/* edma_tx_fill_desc() enables TSO from skb_is_gso() alone, not from
+	 * ip_summed, so a GSO skb that is not CHECKSUM_PARTIAL -- a
+	 * GRO-coalesced frame being forwarded, for instance -- has to be
+	 * checked too, or it keeps hardware segmentation and is sliced at
+	 * the same misplaced offsets.
+	 */
+	if (skb->ip_summed != CHECKSUM_PARTIAL && !skb_is_gso(skb))
+		return features;
+
+	l3_off = skb_network_offset(skb);
+
+	/* Only an 802.1Q tag is assumed to be one the parser follows. No
+	 * TPID register is ever programmed, so whether it also recognises
+	 * 802.1ad is unknown, and assuming it does would keep offload on a
+	 * frame whose L3 the parser never reaches; assuming it does not
+	 * merely costs offload.
+	 *
+	 * With a head-inserted tag in front these two bytes lie inside that
+	 * tag rather than being an ethertype, so the test can match on
+	 * rubbish. That stays safe because the parser reads the ethertype at
+	 * the same offset as this does: a spurious 802.1Q match means the
+	 * parser skips the same four bytes and arrives at the same L3 offset
+	 * computed here, and a spurious 802.1ad match is masked through
+	 * vlan_parseable regardless. Do not reason instead that l3_off always
+	 * lands beyond expected -- that is false for a four-byte head tag,
+	 * where both come to ETH_HLEN + VLAN_HLEN.
+	 */
+	if (skb_headlen(skb) >= ETH_HLEN) {
+		__be16 proto = ((struct ethhdr *)skb->data)->h_proto;
+
+		if (eth_type_vlan(proto)) {
+			nvlan++;
+			if (proto != htons(ETH_P_8021Q))
+				vlan_parseable = false;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* An offloaded VLAN tag is not in the data yet: validate_xmit_vlan()
+	 * runs after netif_skb_features(). Unless the hardware is going to
+	 * insert it, it gets pushed inside afterwards and shifts L3 by a
+	 * further VLAN_HLEN.
+	 *
+	 * Leaving nvlan alone when the hardware will insert the tag assumes
+	 * the parser reads the DMA buffer, which such a tag never enters.
+	 * Revisit that, and whether vlan_proto needs checking there too, if
+	 * NETIF_F_HW_VLAN_*_TX is ever advertised by this driver.
+	 */
+	if (skb_vlan_tag_present(skb) &&
+	    !vlan_hw_offload_capable(features, skb->vlan_proto)) {
+		l3_off += VLAN_HLEN;
+		nvlan++;
+		if (skb->vlan_proto != htons(ETH_P_8021Q))
+			vlan_parseable = false;
+	}
+
+	/* The parser accounts for one VLAN tag, no more. */
+	if (nvlan)
+		expected += VLAN_HLEN;
+
+	if (l3_off != expected || !vlan_parseable)
+		features &= ~(NETIF_F_CSUM_MASK | NETIF_F_GSO_MASK);
+
 	return features;
 }
 
